wer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{nx}{\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550529} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c2430c-0c6a-549e-aa2b-e39d8ffb22dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550860} {:answer/id #uuid "b70050b3-2831-538a-98f7-589a10e9520d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{nx}{\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550868} {:answer/id #uuid "b732912b-9781-517d-a1b3-41138feb28b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{nx}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550870} {:answer/id #uuid "d386d763-aaac-543d-9911-63e1963b04ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{nx}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4c2430c-0c6a-549e-aa2b-e39d8ffb22dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47fccdc-c568-5695-8718-41b3bbde7ccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed658204-766e-56cd-a12e-1cf2b14b40f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343391" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=3{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)={x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ \nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}({\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nt))\\text{d}t \\\\ [{t}^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[{\\pi }^{2}t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nt)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ {x}^{3} & ∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
